#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:13
msgid "MCP Guide"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:20
msgid "Captain Social Feed can let AI assistants like Claude, Cursor, or ChatGPT create and manage your feeds through natural language, using WordPress's own Model Context Protocol (MCP) support. This page explains what that means, what it needs, and how to connect."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:28
msgid "This entire feature is optional. If you never connect an AI assistant, nothing about it affects the rest of the plugin. Every setting, feed type, and shortcode works exactly the same either way."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:35
msgid "How It Works"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:38
msgid "Captain Social Feed does not run its own MCP server, and it does not require any dedicated bridge app made specifically for this plugin. Instead, it registers its feed actions (list, create, update, duplicate, delete, and diagnose) as WordPress \"Abilities\", using the Abilities API that's built into WordPress core."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:41
msgid "A separate, general-purpose \"MCP bridge\" plugin then exposes those abilities to any AI client. That bridge plugin isn't specific to Captain Social Feed - the same one works for every plugin on your site that registers abilities, so you only ever connect Claude to your site once, not once per plugin."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:48
msgid "Why this matters:"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:48
msgid "You're never locked into a connector built and maintained by this plugin alone. You can swap the bridge plugin at any time, and any other WordPress plugin you install that also supports the Abilities API will show up in the same AI connection automatically."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:54
msgid "Requirements"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:61
msgid "WordPress 6.9 or newer"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:62
msgid "The Abilities API shipped in WordPress core with version 6.9. On older WordPress versions, the MCP page will show a compatibility notice and no abilities are registered. Everything else in the plugin is unaffected. Check your version under Dashboard → Updates, and update WordPress if needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:69
msgid "An MCP bridge plugin"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:70
msgid "Captain Social Feed doesn't bundle one, so you'll install one separately (see below). This is a one-time setup for your whole site."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:77
msgid "An admin account"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:78
msgid "Every ability requires the \"manage_options\" capability, the same permission level needed to use this plugin's Settings page. AI assistants can never act as a user with less access than that."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:86
msgid "Which Bridge Plugin Should I Use?"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:89
msgid "Any plugin that supports the WordPress Abilities API will work. Two common options:"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:96
msgid "Easy MCP AI (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:97
msgid "Available directly from the WordPress.org plugin directory. Install and activate it like any other plugin, no separate download needed. It includes a built-in OAuth 2.1 connector, so connecting Claude Desktop is just pasting a URL and clicking Approve: no config files, no passwords, no command line."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:104
msgid "Official WordPress MCP Adapter (GitHub only)"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:106
msgid "Maintained by the WordPress core team, but not yet listed on WordPress.org, it's installed from its "
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:107
msgid "GitHub Releases page"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:108
msgid " instead (Plugins → Add New → Upload Plugin). It connects using a WordPress Application Password, pasted into Claude Desktop's own MCP config file."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:118
msgid "This guide is not affiliated with either bridge plugin. Both are independent, general-purpose WordPress plugins. Captain Social Feed simply registers its abilities in the standard way any of them can pick up."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:124
msgid "How to Connect (Easy MCP AI)"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:131
msgid "Install Easy MCP AI"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:132
msgid "In your WordPress admin, go to Plugins → Add New, search for \"Easy MCP AI\", then install and activate it."
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:139
msgid "Turn on the abilities you want"
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:140
msgid "On this plugin's "
msgstr ""

#: src/page/docs/mcp-integration/mcp-integration.jsx:140
msgid "MCP page"
msgstr ""

